  "problem": "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $Q?$\n\n- $\u00acR\u2227(Q\u2227\u00acR)$\n- $Q\u2227(\u00acR\u2228Q)$\n- $(\u00acR\u2228Q)\u2227Q$",
  "worked_solution": "The law that conjunction absorbs disjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nQ\u2227(Q\u2228\u00acR)\u2261Q.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is *not* logically equivalent to $Q.$ This statement cannot be simplified using the absorption laws.\n\n- Statement II is logically equivalent to $Q.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q\u2227(\u00acR\u2228Q) & \u2261Q\u2227(Q\u2228\u00acR) \\\\  & \u2261Q.\\,\u2713\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is logically equivalent to $Q.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\u00acR\u2228Q)\u2227Q & \u2261Q\u2227(Q\u2228\u00acR) \\\\  & \u2261Q.\\,\u2713\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
